findings.body:
The editable gate changed from `memberName` to `member?.expertise`:

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="259-265"}

Expertise is an optional profile field, and most members never set it. With this change, a member who has a name but no expertise can't type in either the main form or a reply form. The comment directly above the line still says the editor should be disabled when the member "doesn't have a name", so the code and comment now disagree.

The new test `Reply form does not inherit expertise from parent comment author` uses a member with `expertise: null` and calls `waitEditorFocused`, so it should be exercising this case.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="264-265"}
        editor.setEditable(!!memberName && progress !== 'sending');
    }, [editor, memberName, progress]);

`ReplyForm` used to send `openForm.in_reply_to_id`. Now it always sends `parent.id`:

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/reply-form.tsx" lines="24-35"}

`parent` here is always the **top-level** comment (see `ReplyFormBox` in `comment.tsx`, line 251). The id of the reply being answered is only stored on the form, in `openForm.in_reply_to_id`, which `openReplyForm` sets at `comment.tsx:110`. That causes two problems:

- **Reply to a reply:** the new comment is recorded as replying to the top-level comment. The "Reply to: …" snippet and the highlight link point at the wrong comment. The existing `testReplyToReply` assertion at `actions.test.ts:452` should catch this.
- **Direct reply to a top-level comment:** `in_reply_to_id` used to be `undefined`. Now it's set to the parent's id. That changes the stored data and can make the comment render as a reply to a reply (`comment.tsx:323`).

This change has nothing to do with the bug described in the PR.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/reply-form.tsx" lines="30-35"}
                in_reply_to_id: openForm.in_reply_to_id,
                status: 'published',
                html
            }
        });
    }, [parent, postId, openForm, dispatchAction]);

`openForm` is optional in `FormWrapperProps`, and `MainForm` never passes it (`main-form.tsx:98`). With the `?.` removed, `openForm.in_reply_to_snippet` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` as soon as the main form's `isOpen` turns true, i.e. when the editor is focused or has content:

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="311-315"}

The package's `tsconfig.json` has `strict: true`, so this line is also a type error (`'openForm' is possibly 'undefined'`).

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="313-313"}
        const isReplyToReply = !!openForm?.in_reply_to_snippet;

The second new test only checks `not.toHaveText('Named Author')`:

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/test/e2e/actions.test.ts" lines="677-680"}

If the `member-name` element doesn't render at all for a nameless member, a negated `toHaveText` on a missing locator can pass trivially, and the test never says what the correct output is. The comment admits as much ("It should either show nothing, 'Anonymous', or prompt for a name"). Assert the specific expected output, such as the name placeholder or the 'Add your name' prompt, so the test pins down the behavior. It would also help to assert that the editor isn't editable for a nameless member, since that's the other path the fallback affected.
